---
Choose the product and then check the host, backend, and model together. A
framework build setting describes which runtime can be included. A server image
specifies its host architecture and packaged runtime. The model and its
precision determine what the selected runtime needs to serve.

# [Build with ZML]($heading.id('build-with-zml'))

Start with the [ZML quickstart]($link.page('zml/user-guide/quickstart')) to run the
pretrained MNIST example on CPU. For another backend, pass
`--@zml//platforms:<backend>=true` to Bazel. CPU is enabled by default.

| Backend | Build setting | Source requirements |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CPU | `cpu` | Use the toolchain and host target selected by the checkout. |
| NVIDIA CUDA | `cuda` | See the CUDA plugin and driver requirements for your build target. |
| AMD ROCm, Google TPU, AWS Neuron, Intel oneAPI, Furiosa | `rocm`, `tpu`, `neuron`, `oneapi`, `furiosa` | Linux x86-64 in the upstream build definitions. |
| Apple Metal | `metal` | macOS on Apple Silicon. |

See the [ZML platform build definitions](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/platforms/BUILD.bazel)
at the revision used by the quickstart. The checkout determines which backends
and models its examples recognize.

# [Serve models with LLMD]($heading.id('serve-models-with-llmd'))

| Platform | Host architecture | Distribution |
| --- | --- | --- |
| NVIDIA CUDA | Linux amd64 or arm64 | `zmlai/llmd:cuda` · [Configure]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/quickstart').unsafeRef('lfm3-fp8-cuda')) |
| AMD ROCm | Linux amd64 | `zmlai/llmd:rocm` · [Configure]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/quickstart').unsafeRef('lfm3-fp8-rocm')) |
| Intel oneAPI | Linux amd64 | `zmlai/llmd:oneapi` · [Configure]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/quickstart').unsafeRef('lfm3-oneapi')) |
| Google TPU | Google TPU VM, Linux amd64 | `zmlai/llmd:tpu` · [Configure]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/quickstart').unsafeRef('lfm3-tpu')) |
| Apple Metal | macOS Tahoe or later on Apple Silicon | `brew install zml/zml/llmd` · [Configure]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/quickstart').unsafeRef('lfm3-fp8-metal')) |
| Furiosa RNGD | Linux amd64 | `zmlai/llmd:furiosa` · [Configure]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/quickstart').unsafeRef('lfm3-furiosa')) · [Container device setup](https://developer.furiosa.ai/latest/en/cloud_native_toolkit/container.html). |

Check the architectures available for each backend on [Docker Hub](https://hub.docker.com/r/zmlai/llmd/tags).

# [Model examples]($heading.id('checkpoint-examples'))

These combinations come from the upstream LLMD release recipes. See
[Model compatibility]($link.page('llmd/user-guide/model-compatibility')) for additional models and
backend-specific launch recipes.

| Model | Precision | Backends in the published recipe | Evidence |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| [`LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8`](https://huggingface.co/LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B-FP8) | FP8 | CUDA, ROCm, Apple Metal | [LLMD 20261029.0](https://zml.ai/posts/llmd-20261029.0/#try-it-today) |
| [`LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B`](https://huggingface.co/LiquidAI/LFM3-1.7B) | BF16 | Intel oneAPI, Google TPU | [LLMD 20261029.0](https://zml.ai/posts/llmd-20261029.0/#try-it-today) |

# [Verification status]($heading.id('verification-status'))

The tables are source-reviewed. Local hardware runs for the quickstart recipes
are pending. When documenting a run, record the ZML commit or LLMD version,
along with the host OS and device, model revision and precision, and observed
prediction or completion.
